      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To establish the factors considered by adult regular dental attenders to be the most important in choosing to stay with a particular dentist or practice."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "An anonymous questionnaire to be completed by patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "General dental practices in England and Wales with a significant proportion of patients seen under some form of private contract."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS AND METHODS",
          "content": "13 practices in England and Wales were selected to represent a regional cross-section of the country. Up to 100 successive patients visiting each practice during the summer of 1995 were invited to complete the questionnaire. 1003 questionnaires were returned."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Notwithstanding regional, gender and socio-economic status related differences the most important factors were 'care and attention' rated as very important by 90% of respondents, 'pain control, 'dentist puts you at ease ', 'safety conscious' all rated as very important by 79-82% of respondents, and 'explanation of treatments' rated as very important by 73% of respondents. The importance attached to the dentist being 'safety conscious' suggests a change in emphasis from earlier studies."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The factors rated most important reflect the dentist's behaviour and personal skills in devoting time and attention to the patient."
        }
      ]},
